window.docContents[102].resolve({"/Foundations___-system___-part___-and-the-ontological-square/The-mereology-Dybk___r-implies/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Foundations___-system___-part___-and-the-ontological-square--The-mereology-Dybk___r-implies":{"contents":"The word mereology does not occur in Dybkær's text. The structure does, at two levels,\nand the levels never meet.\n\nAt the level of concepts, the vehicle is the partitive relation that ISO 704 and\nISO 1087-1 provide and Fig. 2.1 (item 2) sets in notation: a comprehensive concept\nover its partitive concepts, beside the generic and associative relations. Dybkær puts\nit to work as a relation among universals. The quantity taxonomy itself is drawn with\npartitive rather than generic edges (Fig. 6.9); the representation of a unitary quantity\nvalue has the numerical value and the metrological unit as coordinate concepts in\npartitive relation to it (Fig. 9.25); a property-value scale stands in partitive\nrelation to ⟨property value⟩ (§11.8). And he rules with it: ⟨property⟩ over\n⟨property value⟩ cannot be comprehensive-over-partitive, because a property value is not\na detachable piece of a property (§9.13.2).\n\nAt the level of systems, the vocabulary is plain part-talk, and it is load-bearing\nfrom the opening of Chapter 3. ⟨system⟩ was adopted \"to accommodate any partition into\ncomponents\" (§3.2, where Bunge is quoted: \"there are no simple structureless entities\").\nThe definition itself is mereological — a system is a \"demarcated arrangement of a set\nof elements and a set of relationships or processes between these elements\" (§3.3), an\nelement being \"any definable part except a relationship or a process\" (§3.3 Note 3) —\nand its worked examples are a six-deep chain of parts: a human being, the blood of that\nhuman being, the leukocytes of that blood, a single leukocyte among them, the DNA of\nthat leukocyte, a given gene in that DNA. ⟨component⟩ is then defined as \"part of a\nsystem\" outright (§3.4), with the complementary part — the chemist's matrix — named\nbeside it (§3.4 Note 2), and the improper case, system and component \"designated\nidentically\", admitted (§3.4 Note 3). Fig. 3.5 splits ⟨object⟩ into composite and\nnon-composite. The relation even carries definitional weight one level up: the minimal\ndefinition of ⟨property⟩ — \"inherent feature of a system\" — is available exactly because\n\"a relevant component is a part of a system and therefore might not need special\nmention\" (§5.5.1).\n\nTwo further facts fix how these two levels must be read, and both are Dybkær's own.\nFirst, they are genuinely two: the partitive relation holds between concepts —\nuniversals — while a component is part of a particular system, and nothing in the text\nidentifies the two relations or maps one onto the other. Second, at the one point where\nthe levels could have been collapsed, he refuses. Asked whether ⟨object⟩ stands in a\npartitive relation to its properties — whether properties are the parts objects are made\nof — §2.23.3 answers that \"the description of a biological object by a set of all its\nproperties hardly means that a duplicate instance can be created by putting the\nproperties together\", and the object–property relation is fixed as associative (§3.1,\nFig. 2.25). That is a refusal of the bundle theory in one sentence: properties\ncharacterize objects, they do not compose them. What is needed, then, is a frame\nthat keeps properties and their bearers in distinct categories related by\ncharacterization rather than parthood, distinguishes universals from particulars on both\nsides, and leaves room for a part–whole relation among the particulars and another among\nthe universals without confusing either with instantiation. That frame exists, and it is\nLowe's.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Foundations: system, part, and the ontological square","header":"8.1. The mereology Dybkær implies","id":"/Foundations___-system___-part___-and-the-ontological-square/The-mereology-Dybk___r-implies/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Foundations___-system___-part___-and-the-ontological-square--The-mereology-Dybk___r-implies"},"/Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM/Property-values/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Property-values":{"contents":"* This work* Dybkær (2009)* ISO 80000-1:2022* VIM 3 (JCGM 200:2012)* VIM 4 2CD* PropertyValue* §9.15* 6.2 (product of a unit and a number)* 1.19 quantity value* 1.24 value of a quantity; 1.35 value of an ordinal quantity; 6.5 value of a nominal property* PropertyValue.kind* §9.15* 4.1 of the same kind* 1.2 kind of quantity* 1.2 quantities of the same kind* PropertyValue.numeral* §16.10* 6.2 numerical value* 1.20 numerical quantity value* 1.25 numerical value of a quantity* PropertyValue.reference* §16.10* 6.2 unit* 1.1 Note 2 (reference)* 1.12 measurement unit; 1.34 ordinal scale* PropertyValue.Comparable* §9.15* 4.1 of the same kind* 1.2 kind of quantity* 1.2 quantities of the same kind* Comparable.refl* §9.15* —* —* —* Comparable.symm* §9.15* —* —* —* Comparable.trans* §9.15* —* —* —* PropertyValue.IsQuantityValue* §16.10* 6.2 (product of a unit and a number)* 1.19 quantity value* 1.24 value of a quantity\n\n* Agreement. A value is a number together with a reference, and it belongs to one\nkind. IsQuantityValue holds exactly when the kind is a kind of quantity, which is the\nscope of quantity value in VIM 3 and of value of a quantity in VIM 4 2CD.* PropertyValue is broader than quantity value. It also represents the value of a\nnominal property. VIM 3 notes that a nominal property has a value but defines no term\nfor it; VIM 4 2CD defines one in a separate entry (6.5). The ISO/VIM quantity value\ncorresponds to a PropertyValue for which IsQuantityValue holds.* The number is an integer. numeral is an Int. VIM 3 1.19 allows any number,\nincluding a complex one. A real-valued magnitude is represented in this work by\nQuantity k R at a suitable carrier, not by PropertyValue.* numeral is not always a numerical value. VIM 3 defines the numerical quantity\nvalue only for quantities that have a unit, and VIM 4 2CD 1.35 says that the numeral of\nan ordinal value is an identifier of a position, not a numerical value. numeral holds\nthe number in every case, including the code of a nominal value. It is a numerical\nvalue in the VIM sense only when the kind is interval or ratio.* The reference is an identifier. reference is a String naming a unit or a\ncategory. In VIM 3 a reference is a measurement unit, a measurement\nprocedure, a reference material, or a combination. This work represents the unit by\nMetrologicalUnit and the procedure by the kind's examination principle; the value\ncarries only the name.* What a value is. VIM 4 2CD defines a value as an individual quantity identified in a\nparticular way. Here a value is a separate record that names a kind; it is not an\nIndividualProperty and has no bearer.* Comparable is stricter and is proved an equivalence. Two values are comparable\nwhen their kinds are equal. The sources say that quantities of the same kind are\ncomparable and state no laws; Comparable.refl, Comparable.symm and\nComparable.trans prove that the relation is an equivalence. Values of two kinds that\nspecialize a common kind are not Comparable at the value layer; that wider relation\nis MutuallyComparable, below.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Standards Reference — PKC beside ISO 80000-1 and the VIM","header":"34.4. Property values","id":"/Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM/Property-values/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Property-values"}});